<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slovenes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Slovenes are a South Slavic people living mainly in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> they lived in the Austrian crown lands such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. Accounts of the Austro-Hungarian Empire list them among the nationalities of the multinational empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slovenes.jpg" alt="Slovenes" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jurij Šubic · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jurij_%C5%A0ubic_-_Volkstrachten_aus_Krain-Slowenen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the 6th century CE Slavic people settled the region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and the Adriatic Sea in two waves: the first came from the Moravian lands around 550, and the second, from the southeast, followed the migration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 568. From 623 to 658 Slavic peoples between the upper Elbe River and the Karawanks mountain range united under King Samo in what is known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samo</a>'s Tribal Union. It collapsed after his death, but a smaller principality, Carantania, remained, centred on present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>. Facing the danger of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> tribes, the Carantanians accepted a union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 745 and later recognised Frankish rule and accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The last Slavic state formation in the region, the principality of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kocel">Kocel</a>, lost its independence in 874. Slovene ethnic territory then shrank under pressure from Germans in the west and the arrival of Hungarians in the Pannonian plain, and it stabilised in its present form in the 15th century. The first mentions of a common Slovene identity date from the 16th century, when the Protestant preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primoz-trubar">Primož Trubar</a> and his followers printed the first books in Slovene and Jurij Dalmatin translated the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> and Emperor Joseph II, reforms included compulsory primary education in Slovene in 1774, and a national revival began. In 1848 the first Slovene political programme, United Slovenia, demanded an autonomous kingdom of all Slovene-speaking lands within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>. It failed, but it remained a platform for later activity, including mass rallies called tabori. The first Slovene political parties appeared in the 1890s, all loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> most Slovenes joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a>, then the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and Slovenes and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. In the territory given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, about 327,000 of 1.3 million Slovenes were subjected to forced Fascist Italianization. In the Second World War Slovenia was divided among Nazi Germany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. After the war it formed part of socialist Yugoslavia, and in 1991 it became independent after a brief ten-day war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most ethnic Slovenes are Roman Catholic, with historical Protestant minorities, especially Lutherans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prekmurje">Prekmurje</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> manuscripts are the first written words in Slovene. Anton Tomaž Linhart was the first to define Slovenes as a separate branch of the Slavic people, in his work of 1791. Between 1880 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the largest numbers of Slovenes emigrated to America, and the largest group in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> settled in Cleveland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Slovenes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire was a mosaic of peoples: Germans, Hungarians, Czechs, Slovaks, Poles, Ruthenians, Romanians, Croats, Serbs, <b>Slovenes</b> and Italians, with large Jewish communities in Galicia, Bohemia and Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It satisfied Hungarian aspirations while leaving the empire's other nationalities, Czechs, Slovaks, Poles, Ruthenians, <b>Slovenes</b>, Croats, Serbs, Romanians, Italians and others, without comparable autonomy, a permanent source of political tension.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ustaše grew out of Croatian resentment of the centralised Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and <b>Slovenes</b>, proclaimed in 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 December 1918 Serbia united with the newly created State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs to form the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and <b>Slovenes</b>, later known as the Kingdom of Yugoslavia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 December 1918 the Prince Regent Alexander proclaimed the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and <b>Slovenes</b> in Belgrade, with his father Peter I as king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carinthian <b>Slovenes</b> and the Croats and Hungarians of Burgenland received minority rights under the State Treaty of 1955, and the Roma and Sinti were recognised in 1994.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
